[tool_use] Agent {"description":"Code review Phase 2b.1","subagent_type":"code-reviewer","prompt":"Review the Phase 2b.1 implementation for the PointFight Reactor project (local-first React/TS/Vite app). Phase 2b.1 ships the LAN companion + WebRTC DataChannel transport with manual SDP paste — intentional throwaway UX (the textareas get replaced by QR in 2b.2). The planning record is `artifacts/decision-memo.md`; the acceptance criteria are in `docs/PHASES.md` § \"Phase 2b.1\".\n\nWhat changed in this iteration (look at `git diff` against `HEAD` — none of this is committed yet):\n\nNEW files (with tests):\n- `app/src/phone/wire.ts` — typed PhoneMessage union (`commit | ping`) + encode/decode/isPhoneMessage.\n- `app/src/phone/wire.test.ts` — 9 tests covering round-trip, type rejection, unknown variants.\n- `app/src/phone/peer.ts` — RTCPeerConnection wrapper exposing createOffer/acceptOffer/acceptAnswer/send/onMessage/onStateChange/close. Default STUN: `stun:stun.l.google.com:19302`. Test-friendly via `peerConnectionFactory` injection.\n- `app/src/phone/peer.test.ts` — 14 tests with a FakePeerConnection stub covering both offerer + answerer state machines, STUN config, send/onMessage routing, invariants (single-role per peer).\n- `app/src/phone/PhoneApp.tsx` — phone-side root: paste offer, generate answer, manual \"Send commit\" button.\n- `app/src/ui/PairScreen.tsx` — laptop-side pairing UI: generate offer, paste answer, show last-commit display.\n- `app/verify-phase-2b1.mjs` — Playwright manual-QA driver with C1–C4 gate checks + C5 best-effort WebRTC.\n\nMODIFIED files:\n- `app/src/main.tsx` — route swap on `location.pathname.startsWith('/phone')`.\n- `app/src/App.tsx` — added `'pair'` to IdleView; PairScreen route.\n- `app/src/store/settings-types.ts` — added optional `laptopLanIp?: string` to SettingsRecord.\n- `app/src/store/settings.ts` — pass-through for laptopLanIp in load/save.\n- `app/src/store/settings.test.ts` — new test for the laptopLanIp round-trip.\n- `app/src/ui/SettingsScreen.tsx` — new \"Phone sensor (Phase 2b.1)\" section with LAN-IP field + \"Pair phone\" button (auto-saves settings before navigating).\n- `app/vite.config.ts` — `server.host: true` + `preview.host: true` so the phone can reach the laptop on LAN.\n- `docs/QUALITY_GATES.md` — one-line \"Browser-API limitations (Phase 2b)\" footnote codifying the testing-gate concession from `artifacts/decision-memo.md` §B2.\n\nVerification done:\n- 129/129 vitest tests pass (was 105 at Phase 5 baseline; +24 new).\n- `npm run lint` clean.\n- `npm run typecheck` clean.\n- `npm run build` clean. Bundle 349.80 KB JS / 107.55 KB gzip (+2.26 KB gzip vs 105.29 baseline; budget +20).\n- `node verify-phase-2b1.mjs` (running against `npm run dev`): gate_checks_pass=true (C1 /phone renders, C2 / renders, C3 LAN IP round-trips, C4 Pair view + phone URL shown). C5 best-effort headless WebRTC handshake fails as expected — covered by manual QA per the testing-gate concession.\n\nReview focus, in priority order:\n\n1. **`phone/peer.ts`** — this is the only piece that survives past 2b.1 (PairScreen + the textareas are throwaway). State machine: idle → gathering_offer → awaiting_answer → connecting → connected, OR idle → gathering_answer → connecting → connected. Look for: missing event listener teardown, race conditions in `waitForIceComplete` (handler is set on `pc.onicegatheringstatechange` — does the resolver run if state was already `complete` when we awaited?), correctness of role enforcement (`createOffer` then `acceptOffer` should reject; covered by one test — anything missed?), `pc.onconnectionstatechange` logic that maps to PeerState.\n\n2. **`phone/wire.ts`** — small surface, but it's the wire contract for 2b.2+. Anything we'd regret about adding `ping` as a variant if 2b.3 needs `calibration` / `sample` messages later? The `KNOWN_TYPES` Set approach — is that the right shape, or should it be a discriminated union with per-type validators?\n\n3. **Test sufficiency** — does the test for \"transitions to connected when DataChannel opens\" actually catch the transition that the production code performs? Per-state transition coverage. Anything in `peer.ts` that the tests do not exercise that would silently break?\n\n4. **DRY/duplication** — `PhoneApp.tsx` and `PairScreen.tsx` both bind a peer, both have state/err/textarea patterns. Is the duplication acceptable for two screens that have *opposite roles in a one-time pairing handshake*, or is there a real abstraction here? Don't extract just because they look similar; flag only if there's a clear shared invariant.\n\n5. **Settings round-trip** — `loadSettings` and `saveSettings` both list field names twice now. If a Phase 2b.3 field is added later, it has to be listed in four places. Is this an existing DRY hole the memo's \"ProfileRecord vs SettingsRecord\" decision should have addressed?\n\n6. **`main.tsx` route swap** — a single string-prefix check. Edge cases: `/phone-something`, `/phone/sub`, `/phones`. Is `/phone-something` matching correct behavior or a bug?\n\n7. **Security / leakage** — `PairScreen` puts the LAN IP into the rendered DOM as a phone-URL hint. PhoneApp reads window.location and a user-pasted SDP. Anything that should be sanitized?\n\nOutput format:\n- **Blocking concerns** (with proposed remediation each)\n- **Non-blocking observations** (note inline, optionally apply if cheap)\n- **Approval verdict**: APPROVE / APPROVE-WITH-CHANGES / REJECT, plus the conditions if APPROVE-WITH-CHANGES.\n\nKeep total response under ~700 words. The reviewer's verdict gates whether `artifacts/phase-approval.json` is written for phase-2b.1."}
